    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d549">

                <pb facs="0829" n="796" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v23/pg796"/>

                <head>549. Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in the Congo<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d549-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1967-09-15T22:36:00+00:00">September 15,
                            1967, 2236Z</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>38356. 1. We are encouraged by developments reported Kinshasa’s 4201<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d549-f2"/> and 4203.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d549-f3"/> OAU seizure
                    leadership on question peaceful evac of mercs<anchor n="4" xml:id="d549-f4"/>
                    gives promise problem can be grappled with and that Congo’s neighbors, as well
                    as Mobutu, will receive political cover they need. Realize that, of course,
                    there many problems still to be faced and that, ultimately, merc agreement
                    needed. Still some unknowns and would appreciate comments.</p>
                <p>2. What is ICRC initial reaction? Has it been informed and by whom? Will it
                    receive formal request to act from OAU? Does it still believe that SYG must make
                    formal request or does it share our view that this need overtaken by OAU
                    action?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>Rusk</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d549-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d549-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Files 1967–69, POL 23–9 THE CONGO.
                    Secret; Immediate; Limdis. Drafted by Haverkamp and Brown, cleared by Arthur R.
                    Day in IO, and approved by Brown. Also sent to Brussels and the Mission in
                    Geneva, and repeated to USUN, CINCSTRIKE, Kigali, Bujumbura, Lusaka, and
                    Bangui.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d549-f2" n="2" xml:id="d549-f2-content">
                <p>Telegram 4201 from Kinshasa, September 15, reported on a meeting of African
                    leaders commissioned by the OAU to implement the September 13 OAU resolution on
                    peaceful evacuation of the mercenaries. After some pressure from the other
                    Chiefs of State, Rwandan President Kayibanda agreed to let the mercenaries be
                    evacuated via Rwanda under the auspices of the ICRC, Zambian President Kaunda
                    offered the use of two C–130s for the evacuation, and Central African Republic
                    President Bokassa offered troops for security during the evacuation. (Ibid.)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d549-f3" n="3" xml:id="d549-f3-content">
                <p>Dated September 15. (Ibid.)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d549-f4" n="4" xml:id="d549-f4-content">
                <p>The fourth meeting of the OAU Heads of State took place in Kinshasa September
                    11–14. On September 13, the Organization of African Unity adopted a resolution
                    calling on the mercenaries to depart under the supervision of “an international
                    body” and pledging assistance to the Congo in the event the mercenaries did not
                    take advantage of the offer.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
